lly. This has led to increased use of third-party hosting services. However, in this setting, there is a lack of guarantees on the computation performed by the inference provider. For example, a dishonest provider may replace an expensive large model with a cheaper-to-run weaker model and return the results from the weaker model to the user. Existing tools to verify inference typically rely on methods from cryptography such as zero-knowledge proofs (ZKPs), but these add significant computational overhead, and remain infeasible for use for large models. In this work, we develop a new insight—that given a method for performing private LLM inference, one can obtain forms of verified inference at marginal extra cost. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://eprint-classic.github.io/2026.html)\nciteturn4search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today;   * 2026/2435 (PDF)     TenseUp: Doubly-Efficient Lookup Arguments from Tensor Codes and Applications to Dynamic SNARKs ...   * 2026/2097 (PDF)     End-to-End Hard-Label Cryptanalytic Model Extraction Using Efficient Sign Recovery ...   * 2026/1688 (PDF)     Hell’s Bells: A Neural Network Pipeline for Ternary Fast Matrix Multiplication Algorithms\n    Runze Zhao, Siqi Lu, Yijian Liu, Zhaoxuan Li, Kaibo Liu, Hongbo Liu, Qingjun Yuan, Yongjuan Wang, Xianhui Lu\n  * 2026/2584 (PDF)     SHAKEing SLH-DSA with Non-profiled SCAs\n    Rafael Carrera Rodriguez, Jad Zahreddine, André Monthieu, Aurélien Vasselle, Pierre-Yvan Liardet\n  * 2026/2583 (PDF)     Rounded Gaussians are Discrete Gaussians\n    Corentin Jeudy\n  * 2026/2582 (PDF)     SoK: Design-Level Backdoors in Block Ciphers Constructions, Detection, and Key Recovery\n    Shah Fahd\n  * 2026/2581 (PDF)     Post-Quantum Sublinear Lookup Arguments from Polynomial Preprocessing\n    Adarsh Basumata, Chaya Ganesh, Varun Narayanan, Sikhar Patranabis, Surbhi Shaw, Nitin Singh\n  * 2026/2580 (PDF)     Table-Free Constant-Work BCH Decodin