bmitted: Oct 2026 | Updated: Oct 2026 ... Quantization reduces the memory and energy needed to serve large language models (LLMs) and AI agents for coding and data analysis can now run on locally hosted LLMs.\nRAQ-MLA: Role-Aware Quantization for Multi-Head Latent Attention Caches\n\nTianqi Chen, Xunchao Li, Linchuan Xie et al.\n\narXiv:2610.33625\n\nAuthors: Tianqi Chen, Xunchao Li, Linchuan Xie, Guanghua Yu, Jianchen Zhu, Peisong Wang, Jian Cheng\n\nSubmitted: Oct 2026 | Updated: Oct 2026\n\nMulti-Head Latent Attention (MLA) has been adopted by recent frontier large language models. Instead of per-head keys and values, MLA caches a compressed KV latent that is up-projected into keys and values at decode time, together with a narrow positional key that carries position information into the attention score. Existing KV-cache quantization methods are designed around the keys and values of standard attention and do not account for what MLA actually caches, which costs accuracy at low bit widths. We analyze the MLA cache both in activation distribution and in how quantization error propagates to the attention output, and show that the two cached tensors play different roles: the latent mainly supplies the values, whereas the positional key dominates the attention score. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nzkLLMPoT: Efficient Zero Knowledge Proof of Training for Large Language Models — Lacuna (https://lacuna.tiptreesystems.com/work/zkllmpot-efficient-zero-knowledge-proof-of-training)\nciteturn4search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Junkai Liang, Zhanpeng Guo, Pengfei Wu, Qingni Shen, Jiaheng Zhang, Zhonghai Wu, Haiyang Xue, Shengfang Zhai arXiv 2026 ... zkLLMPoT is a zero-knowledge framework that allows auditors to verify the properties of a trained Large Language Model (LLM) without ever seeing the private weights or training data. ... They also employ signed fixed-point quantization to handle the math efficiently wi